{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"birdlike-acrid-waterdogs","creation_channel":"python","description":"Reported by a rebar client (report archived at `~/rebar-archived-child-claim-bug.md` on the\noperator host) against `nava-rebar 0.10.1` (source build at `f0cf30a0e` + `bcb735630`) with\n`verify.require_plan_review_for_claim = true`. **The divergence is reproduced in current\n`main`**, so this is not fixed by upgrading.\n\n## Symptom\n\nWhen a container ticket has a child whose status is `archived`, `rebar review-plan <container>`\nsigns a material fingerprint computed over a child set that **includes** the archived child,\nwhile the claim gate recomputes the fingerprint over a child set that **excludes** it. The two\ncan never agree, so every `review-plan` → `claim` sequence fails with:\n\n```\nError: cannot start work on <id>: the plan was materially edited since review\n(description/AC/file_impact/children changed).\n```\n\nRe-running `review-plan` cannot break the loop: each new attestation re-signs the same\nlarger basis the gate will again recompute smaller. The recovery the error itself prints\n(\"run the plan review to earn an attestation, then start work\") is non-terminating for this\nticket shape.\n\n## Impact\n\nAny container that has ever had a child archived — a normal board outcome when a child is\nsuperseded or found to be a duplicate — becomes permanently unclaimable through the supported\npath. The only exits are `claim --force` (which discards the very gate the config exists to\nenforce) or mutating the board to detach/unarchive the child. The message is also actively\nmisleading: it reports a plan edit that did not happen, sending the operator to hunt a phantom\ndescription change.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n1. Create an epic with N children, each with a `file_impact`.\n2. Archive one child. It keeps `parent_id=<epic>`, gains `status=archived`.\n3. `rebar review-plan <epic>` → `PASS`, signed.\n4. `rebar claim <epic>` → fails with \"the plan was materially edited since review\".\n5. Repeat 3–4 any number of times; identical failure, no edit in between.\n\n## Reporter's evidence (measured, not inferred)\n\nEpic `5bb5-2a98-b054-4d66` in the reporter's store: 12 live children plus archived child\n`fb45-e43c-f0cb-4f5d` (`parent_id=5bb5-2a98-b054-4d66`, `status=archived`). Both fingerprints\nwere reproduced by re-implementing the basis from `material_fingerprint`:\n\n| child set | fingerprint | computed by |\n|---|---|---|\n| 13 ids (incl. `fb45`) | `084b676f66849bab` | value embedded in the signed attestation |\n| 12 ids (excl. `fb45`) | `236d5fa9f6944e87` | `attest.current_material_fingerprint()` at claim time |\n\n- `rebar._reads.list_tickets(parent=<epic>)` returns 12 deterministically (three separate\n  processes); `fb45` is absent.\n- The stored attestation's `material:` manifest line is `084b676f66849bab`.\n- The epic's *original* attestation (signed earlier, by a different operator) carries 13\n  `plan-material-pin: child …` lines including `fb45-e43c-f0cb-4f5d` — the review side has been\n  enumerating the archived child **consistently**, not intermittently.\n\n## Root cause (confirmed in this checkout, current `main`)\n\nThe reporter narrowed the divergence to \"somewhere below `list_tickets`\" and named a relation\nsnapshot as a likely candidate. That is correct — the two sides do not in fact share an\nenumeration:\n\n- **Gate side** — `attest.current_material_fingerprint()`\n  (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/attest.py:771`) calls\n  `_reads.list_tickets(parent=canonical)`, whose default is `include_archived=False`\n  (`src/rebar/_reads.py:87`) → **archived children excluded**.\n- **Signer side** — the signed generation's `own_material` is computed in\n  `generation.from_snapshot()` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/generation.py:84`) from\n  `snapshot.child_ids`, which `collect_plan_relation_snapshot()` builds by scanning reduced\n  states and admitting every candidate whose parent resolves to the subject and whose\n  `status != \"deleted\"` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/relation_snapshot.py:283`) → **archived\n  children included**.\n\nSo the divergence is the archived-vs-deleted predicate: the snapshot filters only `deleted`,\nwhile `list_tickets` filters `archived` too. `current_material_fingerprint`'s docstring claims\nit is \"matching `orchestrator.material_fingerprint`\" — that stated invariant is what is broken,\nand nothing enforces it because the child predicate is spelled independently at each site.\n\n## Fix direction\n\nExclude non-live children (archived, deleted) from the material basis, and apply that policy in\n**one shared helper both the signer and the gate call** rather than independently at two sites.\nMaterial is meant to capture *the plan*, and an archived child is no longer part of the plan;\nthis is also what the gate already does, so the gate becomes correct by definition and only the\nsigner changes.\n\nDeliberately **not** choosing the symmetric alternative (include archived on both sides): that\nwould make archiving a child a *material edit*, invalidating a parent's attestation for a board\naction that changed no plan content — trading this bug for a subtler one. The reporter reached\nthe same conclusion independently.\n\nNote `relation_snapshot.child_ids` also feeds `related_material` and the per-child\n`plan-material-pin` manifest lines, so the change must keep those consistent with the new basis\n(archived children should stop being pinned), and existing attestations that pin an archived\nchild must not be treated as forgeries — only as stale in the ordinary way.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. A single shared helper defines \"children that count as plan material\" (live only: excludes\n   `archived` and `deleted`), and both `generation.from_snapshot()`/`collect_plan_relation_snapshot()`\n   and `attest.current_material_fingerprint()` obtain their child set through it — no\n   independently spelled status predicate remains at either site.\n2. Regression test: archive a child of a container, then `review-plan` → `claim` succeeds.\n   Currently fails.\n3. Regression test: archiving a child alone does **not** invalidate a container's existing\n   valid attestation (material fingerprint unchanged across the archive).\n4. Regression test asserting the docstring invariant directly: for a container with a mix of\n   live, archived, and deleted children, the signer-side fingerprint and\n   `current_material_fingerprint()` are equal.\n5. `plan-material-pin` manifest lines for the container no longer include archived children,\n   and an attestation signed *before* the fix (pinning an archived child) is handled as stale /\n   re-reviewable, not as a signature failure.\n6. Full suite, `make lint`, and `make typecheck` green.\n\n## Workarounds until fixed\n\n- Detach the archived child from the parent (`rebar edit <child> --parent=null`) so both sides\n  enumerate the same set, then re-run `review-plan` and `claim`. The reporter applied this to\n  `fb45-e43c-f0cb-4f5d`, which is now unparented in their store.\n- `rebar claim <id> --force=\"<reason>\"` works but discards the gate.\n","id":"b7a2-4453-3bbd-4a54","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["plan-review","attestation","client-report"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Archived child permanently blocks claim on its parent: signer includes it in the material basis, claim gate excludes it"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785199692852213001,"uuid":"5815f77d-6cce-45b2-88e4-3d28c9bff489"}